from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('mixed-04','mathematics','linear-algebra','Mixed Revision · Energy, Logic & State','006');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Find the determinant of A=[[1,2,0],[0,3,4],[0,0,−2]].',-6,'Upper-triangular matrix with diagonal 1,3,−2','Find determinant.','Triangular determinant',r'\det A=\prod_i a_{ii}',r'An upper-triangular determinant is the product of its diagonal entries. | Off-diagonal entries 2 and 4 do not affect that product. | 1×3×(−2)=−6.','A determinant can be negative even when most matrix entries are positive.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A source-free series circuit has L=0.5 H, R=2 Ω and initial current 4 A. Find its total resistor energy dissipation as t→∞, in joules.',4,'Initial inductor current 4 A; no independent sources; R positive','Find total heat after complete decay.','Energy conservation',r'W_{R,total}=\tfrac12 LI_0^2',r'The inductor initially stores ½×0.5×4²=4 J. | Positive resistance causes current to decay to zero, leaving no magnetic energy. | With no source or other storage, all initial energy becomes resistor heat: 4 J.','The resistor value changes dissipation rate, not total initial stored energy.',subject='networks',topic='transient-analysis',unit='J')
b.n(r'For F(A,B,C)=AB+ĀC, evaluate F when A=0, B=1, C=1.',1,'Boolean inputs 0,1,1; OR represented by +','Evaluate the Boolean output.','Multiplexer Boolean form',r'F=AB+\bar AC',r'A=0 makes AB=0 and Ā=1. | The second term is 1×C=1. | OR of 0 and 1 is 1. This is a 2:1 selector controlled by A.','Boolean addition is OR, not ordinary integer addition.',subject='digital',topic='boolean-algebra',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'An NMOS in ideal square-law saturation has drain current 2 mA and overdrive 0.5 V. Find gm in mS.',8,'Saturation; no channel-length modulation; ID=2 mA; Vov=0.5 V','Find incremental transconductance.','MOS square-law slope',r'g_m=2I_D/V_{ov}',r'From ID=βVov²/2, differentiation gives gm=βVov=2ID/Vov. | Substitute 2×2 mA/0.5 V. | gm=8 mS.','Do not use the BJT expression IC/VT for a MOSFET.',subject='devices',topic='mosfet',unit='mS')
b.m(r'An ideal op-amp is used open-loop as a comparator. If V+ > V− by a finite amount, its output moves toward:','A',['Positive saturation','Exactly zero','A negative supply rail necessarily','An ideal virtual ground at both inputs'],'Open-loop comparator; positive differential input','Identify output polarity.','Open-loop comparator sign',r'V_o=A(V_+-V_-),\quad A>0',r'A positive differential input creates a positive output demand. | Without negative feedback, large open-loop gain drives the output toward the positive limit. | Option A is correct; the virtual-short rule does not apply in this saturated mode.','Do not use a linear-feedback constraint for an open-loop comparator.',subject='analog',topic='operational-amplifiers')
b.n(r'For xdot=Ax with A=[[−2,1],[0,−3]], find the slowest natural exponential decay rate magnitude in s⁻¹.',2,'Triangular state matrix; stable real eigenvalues','Find the least-negative pole magnitude.','Dominant state mode',r'\lambda(A)=\{-2,-3\}',r'The eigenvalues of this triangular matrix are −2 and −3. | The mode e⁻²ᵗ decays more slowly than e⁻³ᵗ. | The slow decay-rate magnitude is 2 s⁻¹.','The smallest magnitude negative real pole dominates the long-time decay.',subject='control',topic='state-space-analysis',unit='s⁻¹')
b.n(r'For a conventional AM waveform with envelope maximum 12 V and minimum 4 V, find modulation index m.',0.5,'Envelope extrema 12 and 4; nonovermodulated sinusoidal AM','Find the modulation index from the envelope.','AM envelope measurement',r'm=\frac{E_{max}-E_{min}}{E_{max}+E_{min}}',r'Envelope extrema are Ac(1+m) and Ac(1−m). | Their difference is 8 V and sum is 16 V. | The ratio is 8/16=0.5.','Use sum in the denominator, not the maximum alone.',subject='communications',topic='analog-communication')
b.n(r'A TEM wave in a lossless medium has RMS magnetic field 0.02 A/m and real intrinsic impedance 200 Ω. Find average power density in W/m².',0.08,'RMS H=0.02 A/m; η=200 Ω','Find average field power flow.','RMS Poynting magnitude',r'\langle S\rangle=\eta H_{rms}^2',r'The RMS electric field is ηHrms=4 V/m. | Average power density for in-phase RMS fields is ErmsHrms. | The value is 4×0.02=0.08 W/m², without an extra half factor.','RMS and peak field conventions have different prefactors.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='maxwell-equations',unit='W/m²')
b.s(r'For an ideal JK flip-flop at its active clock edge, which statements hold?',['A','C','D'],['J=0,K=0 preserves Q','J=1,K=1 forces Q to 1 regardless of previous state','J=1,K=0 sets Q to 1','J=0,K=1 resets Q to 0'],'Ideal JK next-state behavior','Select valid transition rules.','JK characteristic relation',r'Q^+=J\bar Q+\bar KQ',r'With J=K=0 the formula reduces to Q; with J=1,K=0 it reduces to 1. | With J=0,K=1 it becomes 0. With J=K=1 it becomes Q̄, a toggle rather than a fixed set. | Select A, C and D.','The J=K=1 condition toggles and depends on the previous state.',subject='digital',topic='flip-flops')
b.s(r'For a real continuous-time LTI impulse response h(t), which properties separately guarantee the indicated conclusions?',['A','D'],['h(t)=0 for t<0 guarantees causality','A finite DC gain alone guarantees BIBO stability','A finite-order transfer numerator alone guarantees stability','An absolutely integrable h guarantees BIBO stability'],'Ordinary impulse-response functions; standard LTI definitions','Select valid sufficient conditions.','LTI causality and norm bounds',r'\|y\|_\infty\le\|h\|_1\|x\|_\infty',r'No response before a causal impulse can occur when h vanishes for negative time. | Absolute integrability bounds the convolution output for every bounded input. | Finite DC gain or numerator order cannot exclude unstable poles or other nonintegrable impulse behavior. Select A and D.','A single-frequency gain does not characterize the complete impulse-response norm.',subject='networks',topic='lti-systems',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A bag contains 3 red and 2 blue balls. Two balls are drawn without replacement. Find the probability both are red.',0.3,'Five balls; three red; two draws without replacement','Find the ordered two-draw red probability.','Dependent draws',r'P(RR)=\frac35\frac24',r'The first draw is red with probability 3/5. | Given that event, two red balls remain among four total. | Multiply to obtain (3/5)(2/4)=3/10=0.3.','Without replacement the second probability changes.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude')
b.n(r'Two stable first-order systems H1(s)=1/(s+1) and H2(s)=2/(s+2) are cascaded. Find their overall impulse response at t=ln2.',0.5,'Cascade; zero initial states; two distinct poles','Evaluate convolution of the impulse responses.','Partial fractions for a cascade',r'H(s)=\frac2{(s+1)(s+2)}=\frac2{s+1}-\frac2{s+2}',r'The overall impulse response is 2e⁻ᵗ−2e⁻²ᵗ for t≥0. | At t=ln2, the two exponential factors are 1/2 and 1/4. | The response is 2(1/2−1/4)=0.5.','Transfer functions multiply for a cascade; time-domain responses convolve.',subject='control',topic='transfer-functions',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('mixed-05','mathematics','calculus','Mixed Revision · Frequency, Codes & Geometry','006');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Evaluate ∫₀¹(3x²+2x)dx.',2,'Polynomial integrand; limits 0 and 1','Find the definite integral.','Polynomial integration',r'\int(3x^2+2x)dx=x^3+x^2+C',r'An antiderivative is x³+x². | Its upper-limit value is 1+1=2 and its lower-limit value is 0. | The definite integral is 2.','Integrate each power with its own new exponent and denominator.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A series RC impedance is 4−j3 Ω at a given frequency. Find the power factor magnitude.',0.8,'Resistive part 4 Ω; capacitive reactance −3 Ω','Find |cosφ|.','Power factor from impedance',r'|\cos\phi|=R/|Z|',r'The magnitude is √(4²+3²)=5 Ω. | Power factor magnitude is the real part divided by magnitude. | It is 4/5=0.8; the current leads for the capacitive sign.','Power factor magnitude and leading/lagging classification are separate outputs.',subject='networks',topic='ac-steady-state')
b.n(r'For a 4-bit two’s-complement representation, find the signed decimal value of 1101₂.',-3,'Four-bit two’s-complement encoding','Decode the signed word.','Two’s-complement weight',r'N=-b_32^3+\sum_{i=0}^2b_i2^i',r'The most significant bit has weight −8. | The remaining bits contribute 4+0+1=5. | Total is −8+5=−3.','The same bit pattern interpreted unsigned would instead be 13.',subject='digital',topic='number-representations',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A solar cell delivers 0.5 V at 20 mA under load. Find its delivered electrical power in milliwatts.',10,'Output voltage 0.5 V; delivered current 20 mA','Find positive load power.','Photovoltaic output power',r'P=VI',r'Multiply terminal voltage by delivered current using the stated power-delivery direction. | 0.5×20 gives 10 in V·mA. | The electrical output power is 10 mW.','Delivered electrical power is distinct from incident optical power or efficiency.',subject='devices',topic='optoelectronic-devices',unit='mW')
b.m(r'An amplifier has ideal sinusoidal gain 5 until clipping at output ±10 V. What is the largest sinusoidal input peak that avoids clipping?','C',['0.5 V','1 V','2 V','5 V'],'Output peak limit 10 V; gain magnitude 5; centered zero-DC sinusoid','Find allowable input peak.','Linear range and clipping',r'V_{in,pk}\le V_{out,max}/|A_v|',r'The linear output peak is five times the input peak. | Requiring that peak to be at most 10 V gives Vin,pk≤10/5. | The largest input peak is 2 V. No RMS conversion is needed because both quantities are peaks.','Do not compare an input RMS value against an output peak constraint.',subject='analog',topic='amplifiers')
b.n(r'A stable second-order closed-loop system has characteristic polynomial s²+6s+25. Find ζ.',0.6,'ωn²=25; coefficient of s is 6','Find the damping ratio.','Second-order canonical form',r's^2+2\zeta\omega_ns+\omega_n^2',r'The natural frequency is √25=5 rad/s. | Match the coefficient of s: 2ζ×5=6. | ζ=6/10=0.6.','The constant coefficient is ωn² rather than ωn.',subject='control',topic='time-response')
b.n(r'A rate-1/2 channel code encodes an information stream of 12 kbit/s with no extra overhead. Find the coded bit rate in kbit/s.',24,'Code rate k/n=1/2; information rate 12 kbit/s','Find the channel-coded output rate.','Coding redundancy rate',r'R_{coded}=R_{info}/r',r'Rate 1/2 means one information bit for every two transmitted coded bits. | Divide 12 by 0.5. | The coded stream rate is 24 kbit/s.','Coding rate is information bits divided by coded bits.',subject='communications',topic='error-control-coding',unit='kbit/s')
b.n(r'A wave in air at 300 MHz has wavelength λ. Find the electrical length βl in radians of a 0.25 m line, using c=3×10⁸ m/s.',1.5707963267948966,'Air velocity 3×10⁸ m/s; frequency 3×10⁸ Hz; physical length 0.25 m','Find propagation phase length.','Electrical length',r'\beta l=2\pi l/\lambda',r'The wavelength is c/f=1 m. | The line is one quarter wavelength. | Its phase length is 2π×0.25=π/2≈1.570796327 rad.','Physical length and electrical length use different units.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='transmission-lines',unit='rad')
b.s(r'Which statements about a binary (7,4) Hamming code with dmin=3 are correct?',['A','B','D'],['There are 4 information bits per codeword','There are 3 redundancy bits per codeword','It guarantees correction of every two-bit error','It can detect every two-bit error when used for detection rather than single-error correction'],'Binary length 7; dimension 4; distance 3','Select coding-parameter consequences.','Hamming-code distance',r'r=k/n,\quad t_{corr}=\lfloor(d_{min}-1)/2\rfloor',r'The code has 7 total bits, including 4 information bits, leaving 3 parity bits. | Minimum distance 3 guarantees correction of one arbitrary error, not two. | Used as a detector, a nonzero pattern of fewer than 3 bit errors cannot turn one valid codeword into another, so all two-bit errors are detectable. Select A, B and D.','A correcting decoder can miscorrect two-bit patterns; detection and correction modes differ.',subject='communications',topic='error-control-coding',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'Which functions are even on their real domains?',['A','C'],['cos x','sin x','x²','x³'],'Parity defined by f(−x)=f(x)','Select all even functions.','Function parity',r'f(-x)=f(x)',r'Cosine obeys cos(−x)=cosx and squaring removes the sign of x. | Sine and cubing change sign for nonzero x. | Thus cosx and x² are even: A and C.','Being zero at the origin does not determine a function’s parity.',subject='mathematics',topic='calculus')
b.n(r'An object moves 60 km at 30 km/h and then 60 km at 60 km/h. Find its average speed for the whole trip in km/h.',40,'Equal leg distances; speeds 30 and 60 km/h','Find total distance divided by total time.','Average rate over unequal times',r'v_{avg}=D_{total}/T_{total}',r'The first leg takes 60/30=2 hours and the second takes 60/60=1 hour. | The total distance is 120 km and total time is 3 hours. | Average speed is 120/3=40 km/h, not the arithmetic mean 45.','Equal distances do not imply equal time weights.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude',unit='km/h')
b.n(r'For a real periodic signal x(t)=cos(2t)+cos(3t), find the smallest positive fundamental period in seconds.',6.283185307179586,'Two angular frequencies 2 and 3 rad/s','Find their common repetition period.','Commensurate sinusoidal periods',r'2T=2\pi m,\quad3T=2\pi n',r'The simultaneous phase conditions require 3m=2n. | The smallest positive integer choice is m=2,n=3. | Both conditions then give T=2π seconds.','The fundamental period of the sum need not equal either component’s period.',subject='networks',topic='fourier-transform',unit='s',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('mixed-06','mathematics','vector-analysis','Mixed Revision · Fields, Memory & Dynamics','006');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For scalar field φ(x,y,z)=x²y+3z, find the z-component of its gradient at (1,2,0).',3,'Scalar φ=x²y+3z; point (1,2,0)','Find ∂φ/∂z.','Gradient components',r'\nabla\phi=(2xy,x^2,3)',r'The z derivative of x²y is zero. | Differentiating 3z with respect to z gives 3. | The z-component is therefore 3 at the specified point and everywhere else.','The scalar field value and gradient component are different quantities.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A two-port has z11=5 Ω,z12=2 Ω,z21=2 Ω,z22=6 Ω. With I1=1 A and I2=−0.5 A, both currents referenced into the ports, find V2 in volts.',-1,'Given z matrix; input-referenced signed currents 1 and −0.5','Find z21I1+z22I2.','Two-port terminal equations',r'V_2=z_{21}I_1+z_{22}I_2',r'Use the second row of the z-parameter matrix. | V2=2×1+6×(−0.5). | This gives 2−3=−1 V relative to the specified terminal voltage reference.','The load-side current is negative when it leaves the two-port under the inward-current convention.',subject='networks',topic='network-parameters',unit='V')
b.n(r'A memory stores 256 words of 16 bits each. Find its total capacity in bytes, using 8 bits per byte.',512,'256 words; 16 bits/word; 8 bits/byte','Find total byte capacity.','Memory organization',r'C_{bytes}=N_{words}N_{bits/word}/8',r'The array contains 256×16=4096 bits. | Divide by 8 to convert bits to bytes. | Capacity is 512 bytes.','Word width and number of addresses are separate dimensions.',subject='digital',topic='semiconductor-memories',unit='bytes',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'An ideal MOS oxide has capacitance per area Cox=2 mF/m² and gate area 10 μm². Find oxide capacitance in femtofarads.',20,'Cox=2×10⁻³ F/m²; area 10×10⁻¹² m²','Find total oxide capacitance.','Capacitance density and area',r'C_{ox,total}=C_{ox,area}A',r'Convert the gate area to 10⁻¹¹ m². | The capacitance is 2×10⁻³×10⁻¹¹=2×10⁻¹⁴ F. | Since 1 fF=10⁻¹⁵ F, the result is 20 fF.','Square-micrometer conversion includes a squared metric prefix.',subject='devices',topic='mos-capacitor',unit='fF')
b.m(r'For an ideal differential amplifier, the output due to equal voltages applied to both inputs is zero. This describes:','D',['Zero differential gain','Positive feedback','Infinite output resistance','Zero common-mode gain'],'Equal input excitation; ideal differential behavior','Identify the rejected mode.','Common-mode rejection',r'v_d=v_1-v_2,\quad v_{cm}=(v_1+v_2)/2',r'Equal inputs make vd=0 but generally leave a nonzero common-mode voltage. | A purely differential ideal amplifier responds only to vd. | Zero output from this excitation therefore specifies zero common-mode gain, option D.','Common-mode gain is different from the wanted differential gain.',subject='analog',topic='amplifiers')
b.n(r'A state model has xdot=[[-1,0],[0,−4]]x and x(0)=(2,3)ᵀ. Find x1(ln2).',1,'First state starts at 2; first eigenvalue −1','Find first-state response.','Decoupled state solution',r'x_1(t)=2e^{-t}',r'The diagonal state equation gives dx1/dt=−x1. | The initial state fixes the multiplying coefficient at 2. | At t=ln2, x1=2×1/2=1.','Do not use the second state’s decay rate for the first state.',subject='control',topic='state-space-analysis')
b.n(r'For four equiprobable binary input pairs sent independently through a noiseless two-bit parallel link, find mutual information between the input pair and output pair in bits per use.',2,'Four equally likely input pairs; noiseless deterministic one-to-one output','Find I(X;Y).','Mutual information without ambiguity',r'I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)',r'The input pair has entropy log₂4=2 bits. | A noiseless one-to-one output identifies the pair exactly, so H(X|Y)=0. | Mutual information is 2−0=2 bits per channel use.','Conditional entropy measures residual uncertainty, which is zero for an invertible noiseless mapping.',subject='communications',topic='information-theory',unit='bits/use')
b.n(r'A normally incident wave reflects from a perfect electric conductor. Find the electric-field reflection coefficient at its surface.',-1,'Ideal PEC; tangential electric field vanishes at the surface','Find reflected-to-incident E amplitude.','PEC boundary condition',r'E_i+E_r=0',r'The total tangential electric field at a PEC surface is zero. | The incident and reflected electric fields therefore cancel at that plane. | Er/Ei=−1, meaning full reflection with an electric-field phase reversal.','A full reflected power fraction of 1 does not fix the voltage/field reflection sign.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='electromagnetic-waves')
b.s(r'Which are true for conventional ideal SRAM and DRAM bit cells?',['A','C','D'],['SRAM retains state while powered without periodic refresh','Conventional DRAM never needs refresh','DRAM storage relies on stored charge that leaks','An SRAM latch is a bistable storage element'],'Conventional volatile semiconductor memory operation','Select valid storage mechanisms.','SRAM and DRAM storage',r'Q=CV\quad\text{for capacitive storage}',r'A conventional DRAM bit is stored as charge and must be refreshed as charge leaks. | SRAM uses a powered bistable latch, so it does not require periodic refresh in normal operation. | Both are normally volatile. Select A, C and D.','No periodic refresh does not make an SRAM cell nonvolatile.',subject='digital',topic='semiconductor-memories')
b.s(r'For linear vector differential operators on sufficiently smooth scalar φ and vector F, which identities hold?',['A','D'],['curl(grad φ)=0','div(grad φ)=0 for every φ','grad(div F)=F for every F','div(curl F)=0'],'Sufficiently smooth fields so mixed partial derivatives commute','Select general vector-calculus identities.','Mixed-partial cancellation',r'\nabla\times\nabla\phi=0,\quad\nabla\cdot(\nabla\times F)=0',r'The terms in curl(gradφ) cancel pairwise by equality of mixed partial derivatives. | The same cancellation occurs in divergence of a curl. | The Laplacian div(gradφ) need not vanish, and grad(divF) is not generally F. Select A and D.','These identities require smoothness; do not infer every field is harmonic.',subject='mathematics',topic='vector-analysis',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A square is scaled uniformly by factor 3 in both x and y. Find the ratio of the new area to the original area.',9,'Uniform planar scale factor 3','Find area scaling.','Spatial scaling',r'A_{new}/A_{old}=s^2',r'Each side length triples. | Area is the product of two independent lengths. | The ratio is 3×3=9.','Length, area and volume scale with different powers.',subject='aptitude',topic='spatial-aptitude')
b.n(r'A fixed 4-stage pipeline processes N=10 instructions with one instruction entering per clock and no stalls. Starting empty and completing all instructions, how many clock cycles are needed?',13,'Four equal-clock pipeline stages; ten instructions; no stalls or flushes','Find fill plus throughput cycles.','Pipeline latency and throughput',r'T_{cycles}=k+N-1',r'The first instruction takes 4 cycles to pass all stages. | Each remaining instruction completes one cycle later. | For 9 further instructions the total is 4+9=13 cycles.','Steady-state throughput of one per cycle does not remove pipeline fill latency.',subject='digital',topic='computer-organization',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('mixed-07','mathematics','complex-analysis','Mixed Revision · Detection, Bias & Transformations','006');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Find the real part of (2+j)(1−2j).',4,'Two complex factors; j²=−1','Find the real component of the product.','Complex multiplication',r'(a+jb)(c+jd)=(ac-bd)+j(ad+bc)',r'Expanding gives 2−4j+j−2j². | Since j²=−1, the last term is +2. | The product is 4−3j, whose real part is 4.','The sign from j² changes the real cross term.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A causal sequence h[n]=δ[n]+δ[n−2] is driven by x[n]=n u[n]. Find y[3].',4,'Two impulse taps at delays 0,2; causal ramp input','Find the output sample.','Discrete-time convolution with delays',r'y[n]=x[n]+x[n-2]',r'At n=3 the taps select x[3] and x[1]. | The ramp values are 3 and 1. | Their sum is 4.','The delayed impulse selects an earlier input sample.',subject='networks',topic='lti-systems')
b.n(r'An 8-to-1 multiplexer has input D5=1 and all other inputs zero. Select code S2S1S0=101₂ is applied. Find the output bit.',1,'Conventional binary input indexing 0–7; D5=1','Decode the selected input.','Multiplexer addressing',r'index=4S_2+2S_1+S_0',r'The select code 101 represents 4+0+1=5. | The multiplexer routes input D5. | Since D5=1, the output is 1.','Use the explicitly stated conventional input index mapping.',subject='digital',topic='combinational-circuits',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A diode-connected ideal BJT carries 2 mA at VT=25 mV. Ignore base current and series resistance. Find its incremental resistance in ohms.',12.5,'Exponential BJT current; IC=2 mA; VT=25 mV','Find reciprocal small-signal conductance.','Diode-connected exponential slope',r'r_d=1/g_m=V_T/I_C',r'The small-signal current derivative is gm=IC/VT. | Its reciprocal is 0.025/0.002. | The incremental resistance is 12.5 Ω, unrelated to the DC V/I ratio unless further voltage data are given.','Incremental resistance is a slope, not generally the large-signal resistance.',subject='devices',topic='bjt',unit='Ω')
b.m(r'For a BJT common-emitter stage with an unbypassed positive emitter resistor, increasing that resistor generally produces which small-signal effect, all other bias/model parameters held fixed?','B',['Higher gain magnitude without feedback','Lower gain magnitude with emitter degeneration','Zero input resistance','Positive voltage feedback at the emitter'],'Midband small-signal model; fixed gm, collector load and bias assumptions','Identify the local-feedback effect.','Emitter degeneration',r'A_v\simeq-\frac{g_mR_C}{1+g_mR_E}',r'The emitter voltage rises when current increases, reducing the base-emitter drive. | The denominator 1+gmRE grows with positive RE while the numerator stays fixed. | Gain magnitude decreases through local negative feedback, option B.','The question holds the bias parameters fixed to isolate the small-signal feedback effect.',subject='analog',topic='amplifiers')
b.n(r'A unity negative-feedback loop has G(s)=K/(s+1) with K=4. Find its closed-loop DC gain.',0.8,'Forward K=4; stable first-order plant; unity negative feedback','Find T(0).','Closed-loop finite static gain',r'T(0)=\frac{G(0)}{1+G(0)}',r'The forward DC gain is 4. | Negative feedback divides it by 1+4. | Closed-loop DC gain is 4/5=0.8.','Unity feedback does not imply unity closed-loop gain at finite loop gain.',subject='control',topic='transfer-functions')
b.n(r'A coherent antipodal binary detector has decision means ±2 and Gaussian decision-noise standard deviation 1. Express its BER as Q(a), and enter a.',2,'Symmetric means ±2; threshold 0; noise σ=1; equal priors','Find the normalized threshold distance.','Gaussian-tail detection',r'P_e=Q(d/\sigma)',r'Either symbol mean is distance 2 from the zero threshold. | Normalize that distance by noise standard deviation 1. | The BER is Q(2), so a=2.','Normalize by standard deviation rather than variance.',subject='communications',topic='probability-of-error')
b.n(r'A plane wave in a lossless medium has Epeak=6 V/m and Hpeak=0.02 A/m, in phase. Find the ratio Epeak/Hpeak in ohms.',300,'In-phase peak wave fields; stated numeric field ratio','Find the measured field-wave impedance.','Field impedance ratio',r'\eta=E/H',r'The field impedance is defined as the electric amplitude divided by the magnetic amplitude. | Compute 6/0.02. | The ratio is 300 Ω. This is the intrinsic impedance of the stated medium; it need not equal the free-space impedance.','The field ratio is set by the propagation medium, so do not assume the free-space value.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='electromagnetic-waves')
b.s(r'Which statements about binary CRC polynomial checking are correct?',['A','C'],['Polynomial division uses XOR rather than ordinary carry-based subtraction','A zero remainder guarantees no possible error occurred','A nonzero remainder proves the received word is not divisible by the chosen generator','A generator polynomial must be ignored during checking'],'Binary polynomial arithmetic; fixed nonzero generator','Select CRC mechanism facts.','GF(2) polynomial divisibility',r'r(x)=v(x)\bmod g(x)',r'GF(2) addition and subtraction are both bitwise XOR. | A nonzero remainder violates the transmitted-codeword divisibility condition. | A zero remainder can also arise from an undetected error divisible by the generator, so it is not an absolute no-error guarantee. Select A and C.','Error detection has undetectable patterns; a zero syndrome is not proof of perfect transmission.',subject='communications',topic='error-control-coding',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For a pure complex exponential x(t)=eʲ³ᵗ, which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['Its magnitude is 1','Its period is 2π/3','Its phase is constant for all t','Its real part is cos(3t)'],'Continuous-time complex sinusoid','Identify amplitude, phase and repetition properties.','Euler representation',r'e^{j\theta}=\cos\theta+j\sin\theta',r'cos²(3t)+sin²(3t)=1 makes the magnitude unity. | A 2π phase increase requires time 2π/3. | Its real part is cos(3t) and its unwrapped phase grows as 3t, so select A, B and D.','A constant magnitude does not imply constant phase.',subject='networks',topic='signals-and-systems')
b.n(r'A rectangle has perimeter 20 m and width 3 m. Find its area in m².',21,'Perimeter 20 m; width 3 m','Find the other side then area.','Geometry constraints',r'2(l+w)=P,\quad A=lw',r'Half the perimeter is l+w=10 m. | Therefore l=10−3=7 m. | Area is 7×3=21 m².','Perimeter constrains a sum of lengths, whereas area is their product.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude',unit='m²')
b.n(r'For y″+ω0²y=0 with y(0)=1,y′(0)=0 and ω0=3 rad/s, find the first positive zero crossing time in seconds.',0.5235987755982988,'Undamped oscillator; initial displacement 1 and zero velocity','Find earliest positive zero of its response.','Initial-condition sinusoid phase',r'y(t)=\cos(3t)',r'The initial conditions select cosine with amplitude 1. | The earliest positive cosine zero occurs at phase π/2. | Thus 3t=π/2 and t=π/6≈0.523598776 s.','The first zero crossing occurs after a quarter period, not a half period.',subject='mathematics',topic='differential-equations',unit='s',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('mixed-08','mathematics','vector-analysis','Mixed Revision · Conservation & Constraints','006');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For F=(2x,3y,4z), find div F.',9,'Linear vector field with Cartesian components','Find sum of same-coordinate partial derivatives.','Divergence',r'\nabla\cdot F=\partial_xF_x+\partial_yF_y+\partial_zF_z',r'Differentiate each component with respect to its own coordinate. | The three derivatives are 2,3,4. | Their sum is 9.','Divergence is scalar; do not return the three derivative components as a vector.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A 2 A ideal current source feeds two parallel resistors 4 Ω and 12 Ω. Find the voltage across both branches in volts.',6,'Total injected current 2 A; shunt resistances 4 Ω,12 Ω','Find common node voltage.','Norton nodal conductance',r'I=v(1/R_1+1/R_2)',r'Total conductance is 1/4+1/12=1/3 S. | KCL gives 2=v/3. | Therefore v=6 V; branch currents 1.5 A and 0.5 A sum to 2 A.','Parallel branches share voltage, not equal current when resistances differ.',subject='networks',topic='circuit-analysis',unit='V')
b.n(r'A synchronous path has clock-to-Q delay 1 ns, combinational maximum delay 5 ns and setup time 2 ns. Ignoring skew, find the minimum clock period in ns.',8,'Clock-to-Q 1 ns; combinational delay 5 ns; setup 2 ns; zero skew','Find setup-limited clock period.','Synchronous critical-path timing',r'T_{clk}\ge t_{cq}+t_{comb,max}+t_{setup}',r'The launched value appears after 1 ns, then propagates for 5 ns. | It must arrive a further 2 ns before the next capture edge. | The period must be at least 1+5+2=8 ns. Hold timing is a separate constraint.','Setup uses maximum path delay; hold analysis uses minimum path delay.',subject='digital',topic='sequential-circuits',unit='ns')
b.n(r'A semiconductor has conductivity 0.25 S/cm. Find its resistivity in Ω·cm.',4,'Uniform isotropic material; σ=0.25 S/cm','Find reciprocal conductivity.','Resistivity',r'\rho=1/\sigma',r'Conductivity and resistivity are reciprocal material parameters. | Compute 1/0.25. | The resistivity is 4 Ω·cm in the matching centimeter-based units.','Do not change to Ω·m without the corresponding length-unit conversion.',subject='devices',topic='carrier-transport',unit='Ω·cm',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'An ideal current mirror has matched transistors with equal effective size and equal relevant bias voltages. Ignoring output modulation and base-current errors, its output current equals:','D',['Zero for every reference','Twice the reference necessarily','Half the reference necessarily','The reference current'],'Matched 1:1 ideal current mirror','Choose the copied current.','Matched transistor current replication',r'I_{out}/I_{ref}=\text{device size ratio}=1',r'Matched device laws at matched control bias give equal currents. | Equal effective sizes make the geometry ratio unity. | Under the explicitly neglected nonidealities, Iout=Iref, option D.','Practical output compliance and modulation can invalidate the ideal copying assumption.',subject='analog',topic='amplifiers')
b.n(r'For unity feedback with G(s)=K/[s(s+4)] and K=4, find the repeated closed-loop pole.',-2,'Unity negative feedback; K=4','Factor the closed-loop characteristic polynomial.','Root-locus breakaway value',r's(s+4)+4=s^2+4s+4',r'The denominator is s²+4s+4=(s+2)². | Both closed-loop poles coincide. | The repeated pole is −2.','Open-loop poles at 0 and −4 are not the closed-loop poles at positive K.',subject='control',topic='root-locus')
b.n(r'A uniform 3-bit PCM quantizer has eight levels with equal probability. A fixed-length code labels every level. Find the minimum bits per sample for distinct binary labels.',3,'Eight distinct quantizer outputs; fixed-length binary labels','Find the minimum integer label length.','Fixed-length source labeling',r'2^b\ge M',r'Two bits can label only four outcomes, which is insufficient. | Three bits label eight outcomes exactly. | Thus the minimum fixed label length is 3 bits/sample.','A fixed-length label count is distinct from a variable-length coding design.',subject='communications',topic='digital-communication',unit='bits/sample')
b.n(r'An antenna radiates 8 W while dissipating 2 W internally. Find its radiation efficiency as a fraction.',0.8,'Radiated power 8 W; internal loss 2 W; no reflected-power contribution included','Find radiated/accepted power.','Antenna efficiency power balance',r'\eta=P_{rad}/(P_{rad}+P_{loss})',r'The accepted power is radiated plus dissipated: 8+2=10 W. | Divide useful radiated power by that total. | The efficiency is 8/10=0.8.','Radiation efficiency excludes a separate mismatch factor unless that factor is explicitly included.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='antennas')
b.s(r'For an ordinary ideal inverting amplifier with finite positive input and feedback resistors, which linear-region properties hold?',['A','C'],['Its signal gain is negative','Its input resistance is necessarily infinite','Its input resistance seen by the signal is the input resistor when the summing node is at virtual ground','Its output must have the same phase as the input at low frequency'],'Ideal op-amp; stable negative feedback; low-frequency resistive network','Select valid gain and input-port conclusions.','Inverting input-port resistance',r'A_v=-R_f/R_{in},\quad i_{in}=v_{in}/R_{in}',r'Virtual ground makes the source current vin/Rin, giving input resistance Rin rather than infinite. | The output/input ratio is −Rf/Rin for positive resistors. | Thus the stage inverts phase at low frequency and A,C hold.','Infinite op-amp pin resistance does not imply infinite resistance looking into the whole inverting stage.',subject='analog',topic='operational-amplifiers',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For electrostatics in a simply connected region with sufficiently smooth fields, which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['curl E=0','A scalar potential can be chosen with E=−grad V','The potential must be zero everywhere','The line integral of E along any closed contour in the region is zero'],'Static fields; simply connected smooth-field region','Select conservative-field properties.','Electrostatic potential',r'\mathbf E=-\nabla V,\quad\nabla\times\mathbf E=0',r'Static Faraday law gives zero electric-field curl. | In the stated domain this permits a scalar potential and path-independent line integrals. | The potential is determined only up to a constant and need not vanish. Select A, B and D.','The domain and static assumptions matter when relating zero curl to a global potential.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='maxwell-equations')
b.n(r'Find the median of the sorted data 1,2,2,9,10,11.',5.5,'Six ordered observations','Find the even-sample median.','Order-statistic median',r'\operatorname{median}=\frac{x_{n/2}+x_{n/2+1}}2',r'For n=6, the two central positions are 3 and 4. | Their values are 2 and 9. | The median is (2+9)/2=5.5.','The sample median need not itself be an observed data value.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude')
b.n(r'A real two-state matrix has trace −5 and determinant 6. Both characteristic roots are real. Find the larger root.',-2,'Characteristic polynomial λ²+5λ+6','Find the less negative eigenvalue.','Trace–determinant pole reconstruction',r'\lambda^2-\operatorname{tr}(A)\lambda+\det A=0',r'The characteristic polynomial is λ²+5λ+6. | It factors as (λ+2)(λ+3). | The roots are −2 and −3, so the larger is −2.','For negative roots, the larger algebraic value has the smaller magnitude.',subject='mathematics',topic='linear-algebra',difficulty='hard')
save_batch(banks,'batch-006')
